---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import Img from '../../components/Img.astro';
---
<CaseLayout
  slug="circular-stl"
  eyebrow="Marketing campaign + research · CircularSTL"
  title="Reaching St. Louis: #AroundSTL"
  summary="A social media campaign and advertising effectiveness study for CircularSTL’s proposed Circularity District, a place where the city’s unused stuff stays in circulation instead of in landfills."
  snapshot={[
    ['Client', 'CircularSTL + Circularity District Task Force'],
    ['Team', 'Joselyn Garcia, Maddie Elhaik, Gia Grillo'],
    ['Channel', 'Instagram carousel'],
    ['Study', '16-question survey, 31 respondents'],
  ]}
>
  <section id="challenge" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="The challenge">The challenge</h2>
    <p>
      The Circularity District asks people to think differently about the stuff in their closets. CircularSTL’s own
      symposium reports pointed to a clear gap: the public needed education, outreach, and a shift in understanding.
    </p>
    <div class="callout"><p>“Maintaining consistent, non-partisan, relatable messaging is essential to shift public understanding.” <br /><span class="muted">2025 CircularSTL Symposium Report, Action Area 6</span></p></div>
    <p>Our core questions: what makes a Circularity District feel valuable to consumers, and how can advertising show that value in a way that feels relevant and motivating?</p>
  </section>

  <section id="channel" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Picking a channel">Picking a channel</h2>
    <p>We compared three formats before designing anything.</p>
    <div class="features">
      <div class="feature"><h3>Posters</h3><p>Good recall in high-traffic spots, but geographically fixed. Too narrow for a district meant to serve all of St. Louis.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><h3>Email</h3><p>Strong ROI, but it needs an audience that already exists. Better for retention later.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><h3>Social</h3><p><strong>Our pick.</strong> Geo-targeting, broad reach, two-way conversation, and visual-first storytelling.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p>
      The strategy was anchored in the Ellen MacArthur Foundation’s <em>Marketing Playbook for a Circular Economy</em>:
      make circular behavior irresistible. So we framed the District as a financial win for everyday residents, not only
      an environmental one, and studied Patagonia’s “Don’t Buy This Jacket” and Nike’s “Move to Zero” along the way.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="campaign" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="The campaign">The campaign</h2>
    <p class="quote">“What goes around should come back around.”</p>
    <p>
      The tagline nods to St. Louis sayings like “In St. Louis, For St. Louis” and to resale brand What Goes Around Comes
      Around, where “around” quietly hints at circularity. The four-post carousel follows a rule of four: a relatable
      hook, a statistic, what the District offers, and a call to action.
    </p>
    <ol class="carousel wide">
      <li><Img src="/images/stl/slide-1.webp" alt="Post 1: Let's talk junk. Your stuff is worth way more." /><span><b>1. The hook</b> Reframe how people see their unused things.</span></li>
      <li><Img src="/images/stl/slide-2.webp" alt="Post 2: The average household has $3,100 in unused items" /><span><b>2. The math</b> $3,100 in unused items per household (OfferUp 2025).</span></li>
      <li><Img src="/images/stl/slide-3.webp" alt="Post 3: The Circularity District, where STL can donate, repair and circulate, all in one place" /><span><b>3. What it does</b> Donate, repair, circulate, all in one place.</span></li>
      <li><Img src="/images/stl/slide-4.webp" alt="Post 4: In St. Louis, what goes around should come back around" /><span><b>4. The call</b> Build a circular economy rooted in St. Louis.</span></li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Color did deliberate work: red for urgency on the statistics, green for sustainability and the positive outcome,
      and muted tones to ground it. A cluttered room made the message personal, and bold white type kept it readable on a phone.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="results" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Results">What we found</h2>
    <p>We tested the campaign with a before-and-after survey, sent to WashU students, faculty, friends, and coworkers, April 17 to 21, 2026. Respondents ranged from 18 to 55.</p>
    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat"><b>4.15/5</b><span>average favorability toward the Circularity District</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>3.70/5</b><span>average compellingness score for the campaign</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>~80%</b><span>agreed the tagline conveyed a circular economy</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>10%</b><span>felt the campaign was distinctly rooted in St. Louis</span></div>
    </div>
    <p>
      People called the District itself “great,” “useful,” and “needed.” The $3,100 statistic was the single most
      effective element. The honest gap: the campaign didn’t feel local enough, and several people confused the District
      with Goodwill or basic recycling.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="next" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Recommendations">Recommendations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Lead with financial value.</strong> Personal economic benefit reaches the widest audience.</li>
      <li><strong>Go more St. Louis.</strong> Neighborhoods, landmarks, local data, and community partners.</li>
      <li><strong>Name the difference.</strong> Show why a full-service hub isn’t just another donation center.</li>
      <li><strong>Say less per slide.</strong> One idea per slide, brighter color, bolder stats.</li>
      <li><strong>Give a next step.</strong> A website, mailing list, or event, plus a “Learn more” bar on paid posts.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The part I value most: testing showed where our work fell short, and those gaps became the most useful
      recommendations for CircularSTL.
    </p>
    <p><a class="btn ghost" href="/files/circular-stl-report.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the full report (PDF) ↗</a></p>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>

<style>
  .carousel { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 28px auto; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: 14px; }
  .carousel li { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px; }
  .carousel :global(img) { border-radius: 14px; box-shadow: var(--shadow); }
  .carousel span { font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--ink-2); line-height: 1.4; }
  .carousel b { display: block; color: var(--ink); }
  @media (max-width: 860px) { .carousel { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); } }
</style>
